    "final_answer": "I have successfully completed the task. WolframAlpha computed the integral and presented several equivalent forms.\n\n## Answer\n\nGiven that **g(x) = ∫ x² cos(2x) dx**, the expression of g(x) is:\n\n$$g(x) = \\frac{1}{4}\\bigl[(2x^2 - 1)\\sin(2x) + 2x\\cos(2x)\\bigr] + C$$\n\n### Equivalent forms (also confirmed by WolframAlpha):\n\n**Factored/expanded form:**\n$$g(x) = \\frac{x^2}{2}\\sin(2x) + \\frac{x}{2}\\cos(2x) - \\frac{1}{4}\\sin(2x) + C$$\n\n### Verification (integration by parts):\n\n1. Let u = x², dv = cos(2x) dx → du = 2x dx, v = ½ sin(2x)\n$$g(x) = \\tfrac{x^2}{2}\\sin(2x) - \\int x\\sin(2x)\\,dx$$\n\n2. Let u = x, dv = sin(2x) dx → du = dx, v = −½ cos(2x)\n$$\\int x\\sin(2x)\\,dx = -\\tfrac{x}{2}\\cos(2x) + \\tfrac{1}{4}\\sin(2x)$$\n\n3. Combining:\n$$g(x) = \\tfrac{x^2}{2}\\sin(2x) + \\tfrac{x}{2}\\cos(2x) - \\tfrac{1}{4}\\sin(2x) + C$$\n\nThis matches WolframAlpha's result exactly (confirmed in the \"不定积分\" / Indefinite Integral section along with the alternative forms and the \"展开式\" expanded form).",
